[8.392783, "o", "\"\"\"pybc: a GNU bc 1.07.1 replacement in pure Python.\r\n\r\nUsage: python3 /app/pybc/bc.py [FILE]...\r\n\"\"\"\r\n\r\nimport sys\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main(argv):\r\n    sys.stderr.write(\"pybc: not implemented yet\\n\")\r\n    return 1\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@faaedc7acf5c:/app# "]
[8.797728, "i", "grep -n '^\\|EXPRESSIONS\\|STATEMENTS\\|FUNCTIONS\\|SYNTAX' /app/docs/bc.txt | head -80\n"]
[8.802064, "o", "grep -n '^\\|EXPRESSIONS\\|STATEMENTS\\|FUNCTIONS\\|SYNTAX' /app/docs/bc.txt | head -80\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[8.807874, "o", "1:bc(1)                       General Commands Manual                      bc(1)\r\n2:\r\n3:NAME\r\n4:     bc - An arbitrary precision calculator language\r\n5:\r\n6:SYNTAX\r\n7:     bc [ -hlwsqv ] [long-options] [  file ... ]\r\n8:\r\n9:DESCRIPTION\r\n10:     bc is a language that supports arbitrary precision numbers with\r\n11:     interactive execution of statements.  There are some similarities in the\r\n12:     syntax to the C programming language.  A standard math library is\r\n13:     available by command line option.  If requested, the math library is\r\n14:     defined before processing any files.  bc starts by processing code from\r\n15:     all the files listed on the command line in the order listed.  After all\r\n16:     files have been processed, bc reads from the standard input.  All code is\r\n17:     executed as it is read.  (If a file contains a command to halt the\r\n18:     processor, bc will never read from the standard input.)\r\n19:\r\n20:     This version of bc contains several extensions beyond traditional bc\r\n21:     imp"]
[8.807918, "o", "lementations and the POSIX draft standard.  Command line options can\r\n22:     cause these extensions to print a warning or to be rejected.  This\r\n23:     document describes the language accepted by this processor.  Extensions\r\n24:     will be identified as such.\r\n25:\r\n26:   OPTIONS\r\n27:     -h, --help\r\n28:            Print the usage and exit.\r\n29:\r\n30:     -i, --interactive\r\n31:            Force interactive mode.\r\n32:\r\n33:     -l, --mathlib\r\n34:            Define the standard math library.\r\n35:\r\n36:     -w, --warn\r\n37:            Give warnings for extensions to POSIX bc.\r\n38:\r\n39:     -s, --standard\r\n40:            Process exactly the POSIX bc language.\r\n41:\r\n42:     -q, --quiet\r\n43:            Do not print the normal GNU bc welcome.\r\n44:\r\n45:     -v, --version\r\n46:            Print the version number and copyright and quit.\r\n47:\r\n48:   NUMBERS\r\n49:     The most basic element in bc is the number.  Numbers are arbitrary\r\n50:     precision numbers.  This precision is both in the integer part and the\r\n51:     fr"]
[8.807931, "o", "actional part.  All numbers are represented intern"]
[8.80832, "o", "ally in decimal and\r\n52:     all computation is done in decimal.  (This version truncates results from\r\n53:     divide and multiply operations.)  There are two attributes of numbers,\r\n54:     the length and the scale.  The length is the total number of decimal\r\n55:     digits used by bc to represent a number and the scale is the total number\r\n56:     of decimal digits after the decimal point.  For example:\r\n57:             .000001 has a length of 6 and scale of 6.\r\n58:             1935.000 has a length of 7 and a scale of 3.\r\n59:\r\n60:   VARIABLES\r\n61:     Numbers are stored in two types of variables, simple variables and\r\n62:     arrays.  Both simple variables and array variables are named.  Names\r\n63:     begin with a letter followed by any number of letters, digits and\r\n64:     underscores.  All letters must be lower case.  (Full alpha-numeric names\r\n65:     are an extension. In POSIX bc all names are a single lower case letter.)\r\n66:     The type of variable is clear by the context because all array variab"]
[8.808336, "o", "le\r\n67:     name"]
[8.808362, "o", "s will be followed by brackets ([]).\r\n"]
[8.808435, "o", "68:\r\n69:     There are four special variables, scale, ibase, obase, and last.  scale\r\n70:     defines how some operations use digits after the decimal point.  The\r\n71:     default value of scale is 0. ibase and obase define the conversion base\r\n"]
[8.808449, "o", "72:     for input and output numbers.  The default for both input and output is\r\n"]
[8.808644, "o", "73:     base 10.  last (an extension) is a variable that has the value of the\r\n74:     last printed number.  These will be discussed in further detail where\r\n75:     appropriate.  All of these variables may have values assigned to them as\r\n76:     well as used in expressions.\r\n77:\r\n78:   COMMENTS\r\n79:     Comments in bc start with the characters /* and end with the characters\r\n80:     */.  Comments may start anywhere and appear as a single space in the\r\n"]
[8.808942, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
