[136.103691, "o", "319-            remain until the next number is printed or another value is\r\n320-            assigned to last.  (Some installations may allow the use of a\r\n321-            single period (.) which is not part of a number as a short hand\r\n322-            notation for for last.)\r\n323-\r\n324:     string The string is printed to the output.  Strings start with a double\r\n325-            quote character and contain all characters until the next double\r\n326-            quote character.  All characters are take literally, including any\r\n327:            newline.  No newline character is printed after the string.\r\n328-\r\n329-     print list\r\n330:            The print statement (an extension) provides another method of\r\n331:            output.  The \"list\" is a list of strings and expressions separated\r\n332:            by commas.  Each string or expression is printed in the order of\r\n333-            the list.  No terminating newline is printed.  Expressions are\r\n334-            evaluated and their value is printed and assig"]
[136.104, "o", "ned to the variable\r\n335:            last. Strings in the print statement are printed to the output and\r\n336-            may contain special characters.  Special characters start with the\r\n337-            backslash character (\\).  The special characters recognized by bc\r\n338-            are \"a\" (alert or bell), \"b\" (backspace), \"f\" (form feed), \"n\"\r\n339-            (newline), \"r\" (carriage return), \"q\" (double quote), \"t\" (tab),\r\n340-            and \"\\\" (backslash).  Any other character following the backslash\r\n341-            will be ignored.\r\n342-\r\n343-     { statement_list }\r\n344-            This is the compound statement.  It allows multiple statements to\r\n345-            be grouped together for execution.\r\n346-\r\n347-     if ( expression ) statement1 [else statement2]\r\n348-            The if statement evaluates the expression and executes statement1\r\n349-            or statement2 depending on the value of the expression.  If the\r\n350-            expression is non-zero, statement1 is executed.  If statemen"]
[136.104051, "o", "t2 is\r\n351-            present and the value of the expression is 0, then statement2 is\r\n352-            executed.  (The else clause is an extension.)\r\n353-\r\n354-     while ( expression ) statement\r\n355-            The while statement will execute the statement while the\r\n356-            expression is non-zero.  It evaluates the expression before each\r\n357-            execution of the statement.   Termination of the loop is caused by\r\n358-            a zero expression value or the execution of a break statement.\r\n359-\r\n360-     for ( [expression1] ; [expression2] ; [expression3] ) statement\r\n361-            The for statement controls repeated execution of the statement.\r\n362-            Expression1 is evaluated before the loop.  Expression2 is\r\n363-            evaluated before each execution of the statement.  If it is non-\r\n364-            zero, the statement is evaluated.  If it is zero, the loop is\r\n365-            terminated.  After each execution of the statement, expression3 is\r\n366-            evaluate"]
[136.104066, "o", "d before the reevaluation of expression2.  If expression1\r\n367-            or expression3 are missing, nothing is evaluated at the point they\r\n368-            would be evaluated.  If expression2 is missing, it is the same as\r\n369-            substituting the value 1 for expression2.  (The optional\r\n370-            expressions are an extension. POSIX bc requires all three\r\n371-            expressions.)  The following is equivalent code for the for\r\n372-            statement:\r\n373-            expression1;\r\n374-            while (expression2) {\r\n375-               statement;\r\n376-               expression3;\r\n377-            }\r\n378-\r\n379-     break  This statement causes a forced exit of the most recent enclosing\r\n380-            while statement or for statement.\r\n381-\r\n382-     continue\r\n383-            The continue statement (an extension)  causes the most recent\r\n384-            enclosing for statement to start the next iteration.\r\n385-\r\n386:     halt   The halt statement (an extension) is an executed statemen"]
[136.104194, "o", "t that\r\n387-            causes the bc processor to quit only when it is executed.  For\r\n388-            example, \"if (0 == 1) halt\" will not cause bc to terminate because\r\n389-            the halt is not executed.\r\n390-\r\n391-     return Return the value 0 from a function.  (See the section on\r\n392-            functions.)\r\n393-\r\n394-     return ( expression )\r\n395-            Return the value of the expression from a function.  (See the\r\n396-            section on functions.)  As an extension, the parenthesis are not\r\n397-            required.\r\n398-\r\n399-   PSEUDO STATEMENTS\r\n400-     These statements are not statements in the traditional sense.  They are\r\n401-     not executed statements.  Their function is performed at \"compile\" time.\r\n402-\r\n403-     limits Print the local limits enforced by the local version of bc.  This\r\n404-            is an extension.\r\n405-\r\n406:     quit   When the quit statement is read, the bc processor is terminated,\r\n407:            regardless of where the quit statement is found.  "]
[136.10421, "o", "For example, \"if\r\n408-            (0 == 1) quit\" will cause bc to terminate.\r\n409-\r\n410-     warranty\r\n411-            Print a longer warranty notice.  This is an extension.\r\n412-\r\n413-   FUNCTIONS\r\n414-     Functions provide a method of defining a computation that can be executed\r\n415-     later.  Functions in bc always compute a value and return it to the\r\n416-     caller.  Function definitions are \"dynamic\" in the sense that a function\r\n417-     is undefined until a definition is encountered in the input.  That\r\n418-     definition is then used until another definition function for the same\r\n419-     name is encountered.  The new definition then replaces the older\r\n420-     definition.  A function is defined as follows:\r\n421-            define name ( parameters ) { newline\r\n422-                auto_list   statement_list }\r\n423-     A function call is just an expression of the form \"name(parameters)\".\r\n424-\r\n425-     Parameters are numbers or arrays (an extension).  In the function\r\n426-     definition, zer"]
[136.104223, "o", "o or more parameters are defined by listing their names\r\n427-     separated by commas.  All parameters are call by value parameters.\r\n428-     Arrays are specified in the parameter definition by the notation\r\n429-     \"name[]\".   In the function call, actual parameters are full expressions\r\n430-     for number parameters.  The same notation is used for passing arrays as\r\n431-     for defining array parameters.  The named array is passed by value to the\r\n432-     function.  Since function definitions are dynamic, parameter numbers and\r\n433-     types are checked when a function is called.  Any mismatch in number or\r\n434-     types of parameters will cause a runtime error.  A runtime error will\r\n435-     also occur for the call to an undefined function.\r\n436-\r\n437-     The auto_list is an optional list of variables that are for \"local\" use.\r\n438-     The syntax of the auto list (if present) is \"auto name, ... ;\".  (The\r\n439-     semicolon is optional.)  Each name is the name of an auto variable.\r\n440-     Array"]
[136.104234, "o", "s may be specified by using the same notation as used in parameters.\r\n441-     These variables have their values pushed onto a stack at the start of the\r\n442-     function.  The variables are then initialized to zero and used throughout\r\n443-     the execution of the function.  At function exit, these variables are\r\n444-     popped so that the original value (at the time of the function call) of\r\n445-     these variables are restored.  The parameters are really auto variables\r\n446-     that are initialized to a value provided in the function call.  Auto\r\n447-     variables are different than traditional local variables because if\r\n448-     function A calls function B, B may access function A's auto variables by\r\n449-     just using the same name, unless function B has called them auto\r\n450-     variables.  Due to the fact that auto variables and parameters are pushed\r\n451-     onto a stack, bc supports recursive functions.\r\n452-\r\n453-     The function body is a list of bc statements.  Again, statements are\r\n4"]
[136.104244, "o", "54-     separated by semicolons or newlines.  Return statements cause the\r\n455-     termination of a function and the return of a value.  There are two\r\n456-     versions of the return statement.  The first form, \"return\", returns the\r\n457-     value 0 to the calling expression.  The second form, \"return ( expression\r\n458-     )\", computes the value of the expression and returns that value to the\r\n459-     calling expression.  There is an implied \"return (0)\" at the end of every\r\n460-     function.  This allows a function to terminate and return 0 without an\r\n461-     explicit return statement.\r\n462-\r\n--\r\n631-            character names that start with a letter and may contain letters,\r\n632-            numbers and the underscore character.\r\n633-\r\n634-     Strings\r\n635-            Strings are not allowed to contain NUL characters.  POSIX says all\r\n636:            characters must be included in strings.\r\n637-\r\n638-     last   POSIX bc does not have a last variable.  Some implementations of\r\n639-            bc u"]
[136.1043, "o", "se the period (.) in a similar way.\r\n640-\r\n641-     comparisons\r\n642-            POSIX bc allows comparisons only in the if statement, the while\r\n643-            statement, and the second expression of the for statement.  Also,\r\n644-            only one relational operation is allowed in each of those\r\n645-            statements.\r\n646-\r\n647-     if statement, else clause\r\n648-            POSIX bc does not have an else clause.\r\n649-\r\n650-     for statement\r\n651-            POSIX bc requires all expressions to be present in the for\r\n652-            statement.\r\n653-\r\n654-     &&, ||, !\r\n655-            POSIX bc does not have the logical operators.\r\n656-\r\n657-     read function\r\n658-            POSIX bc does not have a read function.\r\n659-\r\n660:     print statement\r\n661:            POSIX bc does not have a print statement .\r\n662-\r\n663-     continue statement\r\n664-            POSIX bc does not have a continue statement.\r\n665-\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@faaedc7acf5c:/app# "]
